`&slice[i]` must yield the data pointer, not the descriptor address (TRISC codegen)
=====================================================================================

Pinned 2026-04-29. The TRISC `TAddrOfIndex` codegen for a `SliceType`
operand was computing `&slot + i*sizeof(T)` instead of
`slot.ptr + i*sizeof(T)`. The slice descriptor's `ptr` field at offset
0 needs to be loaded; arrays and raw pointers don't (the value already
*is* the data start).

Real-world bite: every helper in `std/container/list` and
`std/container/ring` materialises a single-element heap slice with
`(new [1]T)[:]` and then takes `&slot[0]` to return a `*T`. With the
buggy codegen, the returned pointer aimed at the slice descriptor on
the *caller's stack* rather than the heap allocation. Once the call
returned, any write through that pointer corrupted whatever stack
frame happened to occupy that address — most visibly the saved-LR
slot of the parent call, producing CPU faults at PCs of the form
`0x1_0000_0xxx` (an `stw` writing 1 to the high half of an 8-byte
return address).

The fix is local to `TAddrOfIndex(_, _, PtrType(_))`: when
`array.typ` is `SliceType`, emit `ldd r1, r1, r0` after evaluating
the array expression to read the descriptor's `ptr` field before
applying the index offset.

    module sysl.tests.slice_addrof_index

    callee(p: *int) -> int
        *p = 42
        0

    #test
    test_slice_addrof_index_writes_to_heap_data() -> unit
        val s = (new [1]int)[:]
        val p = &s[0]
        callee(p)
        assert(s[0] == 42, "&slot[0] must point at slice data, not the descriptor")
